Deterministic tests for time-and-randomness code via interface injection. Create /workspace/otp/otp.ts plus tests, vitest as the runner. Start from this untestable version (put it in the file, then refactor it):

export function generateOtp(): { code: string; expiresAt: number } {
  let code = "";
  for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) code += Math.floor(Math.random() * 10);
  return { code, expiresAt: Date.now() + 5 * 60_000 };
}
export function verifyOtp(stored: { code: string; expiresAt: number }, entered: string): boolean {
  return stored.code === entered && Date.now() < stored.expiresAt;
}

Refactor to interfaces Clock { now(): number } and Rng { int(maxExclusive: number): number }, taken by an OtpService class constructor; default implementations SystemClock and MathRng exported separately. Then otp.test.ts with FixedClock (settable) and SequenceRng (yields a scripted digit sequence): generated code matches the scripted digits exactly (e.g. [0,0,7,3,2,9] gives "007329", leading zeros preserved); verify passes just before expiry (advance clock 299_999 ms) and fails at exactly 300_000; wrong code fails even when fresh; two generates with different scripted sequences differ. Also a property: for MathRng, 1000 generated codes are all 6 chars of digits. npx vitest run green.

